.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt HALT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm halt ,
.Nm poweroff ,
.Nm reboot
.Nd stop or restart the machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ss os-simple
.Nm halt
.Op Fl p | Fl r
.Ss os-real
.Nm halt
.Op Fl nfw
.Op Fl d Ar SEC
.Nm poweroff
.Op Fl nf
.Op Fl d Ar SEC
.Nm reboot
.Op Fl nf
.Op Fl d Ar SEC
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On os-simple,
.Nm halt
is the project's program.
It powers off the machine by default.
It requires root privilege because it calls
.Xr reboot 2 .
It does not stop processes or services first.
The kernel flushes the page cache and clears the dirty-mount flag
inside the system call, with scheduling already disabled.
.Pp
On os-real, these names are BusyBox applets:
.Nm halt
halts the machine,
.Nm poweroff
turns off its power, and
.Nm reboot
restarts it.
The standalone shell dispatches these applets before searching the
file system; the project's program is not installed on os-real.
.Pp
The applets normally signal
.Xr init 8 :
.Dv SIGUSR1
to halt,
.Dv SIGUSR2
to power off, and
.Dv SIGTERM
to restart.
With
.Fl f ,
they call
.Xr reboot 2
directly.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss os-simple
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p
Power off the machine.
This is the default.
.It Fl r
Restart the machine.
.El
.Ss os-real
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar SEC
Delay the action by
.Ar SEC
seconds.
.It Fl f
Call
.Xr reboot 2
without going through init.
.It Fl n
Omit the applet's sync call.
.It Fl w
Only write a wtmp record.
This option is listed for
.Nm halt .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl halt
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On os-simple, an invalid argument or more than one argument produces:
.Dl usage: halt [-p|-r]
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr reboot 2 ,
.Xr busybox-init 8 ,
.Xr init 8
.Sh HISTORY
The BusyBox applet descriptions are adapted from BusyBox documentation,
